\section*{Question 4}

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/76679461-42bd-4113-907f-7e334f24faea-1.jpg?height=445&width=668&top_left_y=382&top_left_x=726}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Graph of \(v\)}
\end{figure}
A particle moves along the \(x\)-axis so that its velocity at time \(t\), for \(0 \leq t \leq 6\), is given by a differentiable function \(v\) whose graph is shown above. The velocity is 0 at \(t=0, t=3\), and \(t=5\), and the graph has horizontal tangents at \(t=1\) and \(t=4\). The areas of the regions bounded by the \(t\)-axis and the graph of \(v\) on the intervals [0, 3], [3, 5], and [5, 6] are 8, 3, and 2, respectively. At time \(t=0\), the particle is at \(x=-2\).
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] For \(0 \leq t \leq 6\), find both the time and the position of the particle when the particle is farthest to the left. Justify your answer.
\item[(b)] For how many values of \(t\), where \(0 \leq t \leq 6\), is the particle at \(x=-8\) ? Explain your reasoning.
\item[(c)] On the interval \(2<t<3\), is the speed of the particle increasing or decreasing? Give a reason for your answer.
\item[(d)] During what time intervals, if any, is the acceleration of the particle negative? Justify your answer.
\item[(a)] Since \(v(t)<0\) for \(0<t<3\) and \(5<t<6\), and \(v(t)>0\) for \(3<t<5\), we consider \(t=3\) and \(t=6\).
\[
\begin{aligned}
& x(3)=-2+\int_{0}^{3} v(t) d t=-2-8=-10 \\
& x(6)=-2+\int_{0}^{6} v(t) d t=-2-8+3-2=-9
\end{aligned}
\]
Therefore, the particle is farthest left at time \(t=3\) when its position is \(x(3)=-10\).
\end{itemize}
\[
3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}
1: \text { identifies } t=3 \text { as a candidate } \\
1: \text { considers } \int_{0}^{6} v(t) d t \\
1: \text { conclusion }
\end{array}\right.
\]
\begin{itemize}
\item[(b)] The particle moves continuously and monotonically from \(x(0)=-2\) to \(x(3)=-10\). Similarly, the particle moves continuously and monotonically from \(x(3)=-10\) to \(x(5)=-7\) and also from \(x(5)=-7\) to \(x(6)=-9\).
By the Intermediate Value Theorem, there are three values of \(t\) for which the particle is at \(x(t)=-8\).
\end{itemize}
\[
3:\left\{\begin{aligned}
1: & \text { positions at } t=3, t=5, \\
& \text { and } t=6 \\
1: & \text { description of motion } \\
1: & \text { conclusion }
\end{aligned}\right.
\]
\begin{itemize}
\item[(c)] The speed is decreasing on the interval \(2<t<3\) since on this interval \(v<0\) and \(v\) is increasing.
\item[(d)] The acceleration is negative on the intervals \(0<t<1\) and \(4<t<6\) since velocity is decreasing on these intervals.
\end{itemize}
\[
\begin{aligned}
& 1: \text { answer with reaso } \\
& 2:\left\{\begin{array}{l}
1: \text { answer } \\
1: \text { justification }
\end{array}\right.
\end{aligned}
\]
